### Zenvon Forepot

I still have the potion of invisibility. For now, I keep it and peek into the cave.

There is a cookfire inside. Enough light reaches the occupants for me to count them, which is useful because I cannot see into the dark beyond it. Four bugbears. An ogre. Then I make out one orc as well.

I look for something that makes this arrangement easier to deal with. Nothing presents itself. There are six of them, and four are bugbears. I already know something about fighting four bugbears.

Sister Maela pokes her head in to look. She finds no more than I do. Pip takes his turn, and he spends his attention on the occupants.

“You know what's interesting about this group? The orc.”

The ogre has more of my attention. I wait for Pip to explain.

“He's the weakest of all of these characters, and yet he seems to be the only one that has any authority here. Like, normally, an ogre's in charge. So I don't know, but this seems like a very interesting situation.”

Authority is useful to know about. But I want to know how he confirms it. An orc among larger creatures does not by itself tell me who gives the orders.

“Look, Zenvon. In the military, sometimes most of these creatures respond to whoever's the biggest, so normally it would be the ogre in charge.” Pip directs my attention back inside. “An orc would be bringing them food left, right, and center. But if you look inside, he's the one sitting next to the fire. And everybody's sort of listening to him.”

I look again. The orc sits by the cookfire, and the others give him their attention. I have counted the same creatures Pip has. He has found something in how they sit together.

“Okay, that's a brilliant observation.”

“So, I'm just gonna tell you: that tells me he's the brains of the operation.”

“Hmm.”

Six enemies is one problem. Six enemies who depend on one of them to organize the others might be a different problem. I keep watching the orc.

“So… you know, I don't want to tell you how to do your job.”

Pip has already told me what I have missed. I let him continue.

“My experience is not with these kinds of groups, but in the army, if you kill the leader in this kind of group—it's kind of a mob, kind of a gang—if you kill the gang boss, everybody else suddenly doesn't want to fight anymore.”

“That makes our job interesting.”

“I just wanna throw that out there, boss.”

He offers it carefully. It is still a possibility, yes. The bugbears and the ogre do not become smaller because we identify the orc.

“Or, you know, we could run in there and try to kill them all. Last time we faced 4 bugbears, we had to run for our lives, so I'm not entirely certain that's something we want to do.”

That is a fair comparison. We have already tried the four bugbears part. Adding an ogre does not improve it.

“Well, you make a point, but that's—” I pause, considering the orc beside the fire. “Pretty valid. So, what do you suggest? Do you suggest to kill the orc?”

Knowing whom to attack is useful. Reaching him without having to fight everyone else is the part still missing.

“From this far, or do you…” I look between Pip and the cave. “Wanna confront him? Because if we confront him, like, he's gonna…”

The others are already listening to him. That makes his authority easy to see. It also means he does not have to do much to get their attention.

“How about if he alerts the bugbears and the ogre?”

Sister Maela looks at Pip a little carefully.

“Well, let me have a look at the situation.”
